# The console's install policy (desktop-console plan Task 1.0, design section 5.2).
#
# save-exact: the pinning rule enforced by the client and not only by a test, so
#   an `npm install some-package` writes "some-package": "1.2.3" and never
#   "^1.2.3". The rule itself is test/unit/pinning.test.ts; this is what keeps a
#   later `npm install` from quietly needing that test to fail first.
# engine-strict: the toolchain is pinned, so a Node that does not satisfy
#   engines.node fails the install loudly instead of building an app that was
#   never tested on that runtime.
# package-lock / fund / audit: the lockfile is committed and never disabled, and
#   the funding and audit notices CI turns off are off here too, so a local
#   install and the CI install are the same install.
#
# The install command is `npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`
# (console/README.md), and `--ignore-scripts` is passed on the command rather
# than set here, because npm has no per-package escape hatch: ignore-scripts in
# this file would turn off every lifecycle script of every command run in this
# directory, with no way to allow one package back -- and design section 5.2
# asks for the opposite shape, "an allow-list for the few packages whose
# postinstall is genuinely required", which is a decision made per call site.
#
# What that costs is stated in console/README.md rather than left to be
# inferred, and it is less than it looks: Electron's runtime is NOT fetched by a
# lifecycle script in this setup. Electron 44.9.2 downloads it on the first
# `require('electron')` (its own index.js spawns install.js when path.txt and
# dist/ are missing), and the e2e run now does that once, in Playwright's
# globalSetup, before any worker exists (test/support/globalSetup.ts). It used
# to happen lazily in whichever worker reached electron.launch first, and that
# race -- two workers unpacking node_modules/electron/dist at once -- is the
# `spawn ETXTBSY` flake the global setup fixes. So the e2e path works with
# scripts ignored, and by packaging time (Task 4.1) the runtime is already on
# disk.
save-exact=true
engine-strict=true
package-lock=true
fund=false
audit=false
